[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"OkHttp Interceptors & Connection Pooling in Android Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"OkHttp is an efficient HTTP and HTTP/2 client for Android and Java applications. Two of its most powerful features for optimizing network performance are Interceptors and Connection Pooling. This tutorial covers how to implement both in an Android Java environment."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding OkHttp Interceptors"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interceptors are a mechanism to monitor, rewrite, and retry calls. They sit between your application code and the server. OkHttp supports two types of interceptors: Application Interceptors and Network Interceptors."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Application Interceptors"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Executed before any other interceptors, even before the cache is checked."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ideal for adding application-level headers like authorization tokens or logging outgoing requests."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Called exactly once per call, even if the response is served from the cache."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Network Interceptors"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sit directly between the OkHttp client and the network socket."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Access the final Request object, including any redirects or retries handled by OkHttp."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Skipped if the response is served entirely from the local cache."},{"order":13,"type":"heading","text":"Implementing a Custom Logging Interceptor"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is an example of a custom Application Interceptor written in Java that logs request details and response times."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"public class LoggingInterceptor implements Interceptor {
    @Override
    public Response intercept(Chain chain) throws IOException {
        Request request = chain.request();
        long startTime = System.nanoTime();

        Log.d("OkHttp", String.format("Sending request %s on %s%n%s",
                request.url(), chain.connection(), request.headers()));

        Response response = chain.proceed(request);

        long endTime = System.nanoTime();
        Log.d("OkHttp", String.format("Received response for %s in %.1fms%n%s",
                response.request().url(), (endTime - startTime) / 1e6,
                response.headers()));

        return response;
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Connection Pooling"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Connection pooling automatically reuses HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 sockets to reduce request latency. When multiple requests are made to the same host, OkHttp shares a single socket connection instead of opening a new TCP handshake for each request."},{"order":18,"type":"heading","text":"Configuring Connection Pooling in OkHttp"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can customize the connection pool by passing a custom ConnectionPool instance to your OkHttpClient.Builder. By default, OkHttp keeps up to 5 idle connections alive for 5 minutes."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"int maxIdleConnections = 10;
long keepAliveDuration = 5;
TimeUnit timeUnit = TimeUnit.MINUTES;

ConnectionPool connectionPool = new ConnectionPool(maxIdleConnections, keepAliveDuration, timeUnit);

OkHttpClient client = new OkHttpClient.Builder()
    .connectionPool(connectionPool)
    .addInterceptor(new LoggingInterceptor())
    .connectTimeout(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
    .readTimeout(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
    .build();"},{"order":21,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Share a single instance of OkHttpClient throughout your Android app to maximize connection pooling benefits."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use network interceptors when you need to inspect raw network traffic, such as measuring actual byte sizes or inspecting redirected URLs."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always close your response bodies to prevent resource leaks when writing custom interceptors."}]
